[Comments]
Count=10
1=Snow Pack Structure
2=Several melt freeze crusts are located in the top 20cm of the snowpack. These add strength to the pack while 
3=they are frozzen. The snowpack is isothermal. Once the newsnow has melted into a crust daytime warming will 
4=rapidly destabalise the snowpack by late morning. Good early morning crusts will make for good travel. At the 
5=moment the new snow is insulating the rest of the snowpack. Failures are still occuring at the base of the 
6=snowpack, compression test (CT) hard 25 on faceted crystals size 2-3 (FC 2-3). Once the spring avalanche 
7=cycle resumes the releases are likely to be deep. Most of the new snow that fell on the 25-26th has been 
8=redistributed onto south facing slopes. There was a 60cm soft slab on the south side of parkers ridge (2300m). 
9=There were no new signs of avalanches. Warming and solar radiation will likely destablise the alpine wind slab 
10=produing large avalanches. However today the snowpack stability was good.
